LESSON 7: SETTING UP MULTI-FORM INPUT DATA
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on organizing and managing a larger collection of form
inputs using React state.

You will understand:

• Creating structured form-data objects

• Managing many related input fields

• Using one change handler

• Using computed property names

• Handling mixed input types

• Organizing fields into logical sections

• Using flat and nested form structures

• Validating multiple fields

• Tracking field-level errors

• Resetting large controlled forms

• Creating dynamic fields

• Creating conditional fields

• Building scalable form architectures


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Larger forms appear in many applications:

• Registration

• Customer onboarding

• Account creation

• Profile management

• Address/contact information

• Checkout

• Application workflows


As the number of fields grows, organization becomes important.

Instead of:

Many Inputs
    ↓
Many Independent Handlers
    ↓
Scattered Form Logic


A common approach is:

Many Inputs
    ↓
Structured State
    ↓
Generic Handler
    ↓
Validation
    ↓
Submit


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING MULTI-FORM INPUT DATA
================================================================================

WHAT IS MULTI-FORM INPUT DATA?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

In this lesson, "multi-form input data" means:

ONE FORM
+
MANY RELATED INPUT FIELDS


Example:

formData
│
├── firstName
├── lastName
├── email
├── phone
├── street
├── city
├── state
├── zipCode
├── country
├── company
└── jobTitle


IMPORTANT TERMINOLOGY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson is about:

MULTIPLE INPUTS IN A FORM


It does NOT necessarily mean:

MULTIPLE SEPARATE <form> ELEMENTS


================================================================================
CORE CHARACTERISTICS
================================================================================

• Structured values

• Controlled fields

• Shared update logic

• Computed property names

• Logical form sections

• Validation support

• Field-specific errors

• Reset support

• Submission-ready data


================================================================================
WHY STRUCTURED FORM DATA HELPS
================================================================================

Instead of:

const [firstName, setFirstName] =
  useState('')

const [lastName, setLastName] =
  useState('')

const [email, setEmail] =
  useState('')

// etc.


We can use:

const [formData, setFormData] =
  useState({

    firstName: '',

    lastName: '',

    email: ''

  })


Then:

formData.firstName

formData.lastName

formData.email


================================================================================
IMPORTANT
================================================================================

A single object is useful, but it is NOT mandatory.

Possible approaches include:

• Separate useState calls

• One flat object

• Nested objects

• useReducer

• A form-management library


Choose based on the form's actual complexity.


